9. THE ANGULAR, NOT RADIAL, PERCEPTION LIMIT: TILT REQUIRED TO ACCELERATE
========================================================================
  A multirotor must tilt theta = atan(a_lat/g) to produce lateral acceleration a_lat
  while holding altitude. A body-fixed forward camera of horizontal FoV H sees the
  direction of travel only while theta <= H/2.
  FoV  90.0 deg (half  45.0 deg): travel direction leaves the FoV at a_lat =   9.81 m/s^2 =  1.00 g
  FoV 115.0 deg (half  57.5 deg): travel direction leaves the FoV at a_lat =  15.40 m/s^2 =  1.57 g
  FoV 155.0 deg (half  77.5 deg): travel direction leaves the FoV at a_lat =  44.25 m/s^2 =  4.51 g
  a_lat =  0.50 g =   4.91 m/s^2 -> tilt  26.6 deg (inside 90 deg FoV? yes; inside 155 deg FoV? yes)
  a_lat =  1.00 g =   9.81 m/s^2 -> tilt  45.0 deg (inside 90 deg FoV? yes; inside 155 deg FoV? yes)
  a_lat =  2.00 g =  19.62 m/s^2 -> tilt  63.4 deg (inside 90 deg FoV? NO; inside 155 deg FoV? yes)
  a_lat =  4.00 g =  39.24 m/s^2 -> tilt  76.0 deg (inside 90 deg FoV? NO; inside 155 deg FoV? yes)
  a_lat = 12.58 g = 123.41 m/s^2 -> tilt  85.5 deg (inside 90 deg FoV? NO; inside 155 deg FoV? NO)
  Observed engineering response: the depth camera in [A-20] was tilted 30 deg
  upward above 7 m/s, and [A-26] used a 155 x 115 deg camera. Both are this term.
  Verification: at a_lat = g the tilt is exactly 45.000 deg -> 45.000 deg

